// Fixed questions: Accounting fundamentals. Append only.
B('acc', 'Accounting', [
["lena",1,"First week of my accounting course. What actually is a balance sheet, and why does it always 'balance'?","Think of what the business owns and who financed it.","A balance sheet is a snapshot of a business at one date: its **assets** (what it controls: cash, stock, equipment) and the claims on those assets: **liabilities** (what it owes to others) and **equity** (the owners' residual claim). It balances because every asset has to be financed by someone, either lenders or owners: Assets = Liabilities + Equity. Double-entry bookkeeping records every transaction in a way that keeps this equation true.",["Snapshot at a point in time","Assets vs liabilities + equity","Balances because every asset is financed by creditors or owners"]],
["olena",1,"My accountant sends me an income statement and a balance sheet every quarter. What's the difference between them?","One covers a period, the other a single date.","The **income statement** (profit and loss) covers a **period**, for example a quarter: revenue minus expenses gives profit or loss for that period. The **balance sheet** is a **snapshot at one date**: assets, liabilities and equity at the end of the quarter. They connect: the period's profit (minus anything you take out) increases equity on the balance sheet. One tells you how you performed; the other what you have and owe.",["Income statement = performance over a period","Balance sheet = position at a date","Profit links to equity"]],
["diego",1,"Why do companies need a cash flow statement if they already have an income statement?","Is profit the same as cash?","Profit is calculated on an **accrual basis**: revenue when earned, expenses when incurred, plus non-cash items like depreciation. So profit and cash can differ a lot: customers may not have paid, money may be tied up in stock, or loans may be repaid. The **cash flow statement** shows actual cash moving in and out, split into operating, investing and financing activities. A profitable business can still run out of cash, and this statement reveals that.",["Profit is accrual-based, not cash","Shows operating/investing/financing cash flows","Profitable firms can still run out of cash"]],
["lena",1,"What does 'double-entry bookkeeping' mean? Why two entries?","Every transaction affects at least two accounts.","Each transaction is recorded in at least **two accounts**, with equal debits and credits. Buying stock for cash: inventory goes up (debit), cash goes down (credit). Because every entry has equal debits and credits, the books stay in balance and the accounting equation (A = L + E) always holds. It also gives a built-in check: if total debits don't equal total credits, something was recorded wrongly.",["At least two accounts per transaction","Debits = credits","Keeps A = L + E and provides an error check"]],
["lena",1,"Debit and credit confuse me. Isn't a debit always 'money going out'?","Debit just means left side. What it does depends on the account type.","No. **Debit** means the left side of an account, **credit** the right side; neither means good or bad. Debits **increase** assets and expenses and **decrease** liabilities, equity and revenue. Credits do the opposite. So when a customer pays you, cash (an asset) is debited. The bank statement uses the bank's perspective: your deposit is a liability for them, so they 'credit' your account, which causes the everyday confusion.",["Debit = left side, credit = right side","Debits increase assets/expenses; credits increase liabilities/equity/revenue","Bank statement perspective is reversed"]],
["lena",1,"What's the difference between the journal and the ledger?","Chronological diary vs organised by account.","The **journal** is the book of original entry: transactions recorded in date order, each with its debits and credits. The **ledger** groups those entries by **account** (cash, sales, rent…), so you can see each account's balance. Entries are first journalised, then posted to the ledger. Today software does both at once, but the concepts remain.",["Journal = chronological record","Ledger = by account with balances","Posting moves entries from journal to ledger"]],
["lena",1,"What's a trial balance for?","A list of every account balance at a date.","A **trial balance** lists all ledger account balances at a date, debits in one column and credits in the other. If total debits equal total credits, the double entry is arithmetically consistent. It's the starting point for preparing financial statements and adjusting entries. It does **not** prove the books are correct; some errors don't affect the balance.",["List of all account balances","Checks debits = credits","Doesn't prove correctness"]],
["olena",1,"Is my new oven an expense or an asset? I paid for it this month, after all.","Will it benefit just this month or several years?","It's an **asset** (property, plant and equipment), because it will provide benefits over several years. Its cost is spread over its useful life through **depreciation**, so each year bears part of the cost. Spending that only benefits the current period (flour, electricity, wages) is an **expense** straight away. This split is often called capital expenditure vs operating expenditure.",["Long-lived item → asset","Expensed over time via depreciation","Contrast with day-to-day expenses (opex)"]],
["olena",1,"What does 'accounts receivable' mean on my statements?","Who owes you money?","**Accounts receivable** (trade debtors) is money customers owe you for goods or services already delivered on credit, for example a café that buys bread now and pays at the end of the month. It's a current asset because you expect to collect it within a year. Revenue is recorded when you deliver; the receivable disappears when the customer pays.",["Money owed by customers","Arises from credit sales","Current asset; cleared when paid"]],
["olena",1,"And 'accounts payable'?","The mirror image of receivables.","**Accounts payable** (trade creditors) is money you owe suppliers for goods or services you've already received but not yet paid for, like flour delivered on 30-day terms. It's a current liability. It's a normal and useful source of short-term financing: paying on the agreed due date, not early, keeps cash in the business.",["Money owed to suppliers","Arises from buying on credit","Current liability; a source of short-term financing"]],
["priya",1,"I'm a freelancer. What does 'equity' mean for a one-person business like mine?","Assets minus what you owe.","Equity is your **residual claim**: assets minus liabilities. For a sole trader it's usually called **owner's capital**: what you put in, plus profits kept in the business, minus what you've taken out (drawings). It's a book figure, not cash, and not necessarily what the business would sell for.",["Equity = assets − liabilities","Capital + retained profits − drawings","Book value, not cash or market value"]],
["priya",1,"When I transfer money from my business account to my personal account, is that an expense?","Is it a cost of earning revenue?","No. For a sole trader that's **drawings**: a withdrawal of equity by the owner, not an expense. It reduces cash and owner's equity, but it doesn't reduce profit. Expenses are costs incurred to earn revenue (software, rent, travel to clients). Mixing personal withdrawals into expenses would understate profit, and in tax terms that's a problem.",["Withdrawal = drawings","Reduces equity and cash, not profit","Expenses are costs of earning revenue"]],
["lena",1,"My textbook keeps mentioning the 'going concern' assumption. What is it?","What do we assume about the business's future?","Financial statements normally assume the business will **continue operating for the foreseeable future** (at least 12 months). That's why assets are recorded at cost less depreciation rather than at fire-sale values, and why long-term debts are shown as non-current. If there's serious doubt that the business can continue, it must be disclosed, and if it will definitely close, a different (liquidation) basis is used.",["Assumes the business continues","Justifies cost-based measurement and classification","Significant doubt must be disclosed"]],
["lena",1,"What's 'materiality' in accounting?","Would it change someone's decision?","Information is **material** if leaving it out or misstating it could reasonably influence the decisions of users of the financial statements. Small items can be treated simply (e.g. expensing a €20 stapler instead of depreciating it). Materiality depends on size and nature: a small amount can still be material if it involves, say, fraud or a related party.",["Could influence users' decisions","Allows practical simplifications for small items","Depends on size and nature"]],
["olena",1,"Why does my accountant care when I recognise revenue? I sold the bread, end of story.","When is revenue 'earned'?","Revenue is recognised when you **satisfy your performance obligation**: when the customer gets the goods or service. For a shop sale that's immediate. But timing matters with deposits, subscriptions or catering orders paid in advance: cash received before delivery is not revenue yet; it's a liability (deferred revenue) until you deliver. Correct timing puts revenue in the right period.",["Revenue when performance obligation satisfied","Advance payments are liabilities until delivery","Timing affects which period shows the profit"]],
["diego",1,"What's the difference between gross profit and net profit?","Which costs are deducted at each level?","**Gross profit** = revenue − cost of goods sold (for a restaurant: food and drink costs, sometimes kitchen labour). **Net profit** = gross profit − all other expenses (rent, salaries, marketing, depreciation, interest, tax). Gross profit shows whether your pricing covers direct costs; net profit shows what's left after running the whole business.",["Gross = revenue − COGS","Net = after all expenses","Different insights: pricing vs overall performance"]],
["lena",1,"Why is accumulated depreciation shown as a negative under assets instead of just reducing the asset?","It's a contra-asset account.","**Accumulated depreciation** is a contra-asset: it's kept separately and deducted from the asset's cost. That way the balance sheet shows both the **original cost** and how much has been **used up** so far, giving the net book value. Users can judge how old the asset base is. Reducing the cost directly would lose that information.",["Contra-asset account","Shows original cost and total depreciation","Net book value = cost − accumulated depreciation"]],
["lena",1,"What's the 'prudence' concept?","How should uncertainty be handled?","Prudence means exercising **caution under uncertainty**: not overstating assets or income, and not understating liabilities or expenses. For example, inventory is written down when it can't be sold above cost, and expected losses are recognised promptly. Modern standards stress **neutrality**: prudence shouldn't become deliberate understatement or hidden reserves.",["Caution under uncertainty","Don't overstate assets/income","Balanced by neutrality; no hidden reserves"]],
["lena",1,"What does the 'consistency' principle require?","Can you switch methods every year?","A business should use the **same accounting policies from period to period** (e.g. same depreciation method, same inventory method), so results are comparable over time. Changes are allowed only if required by a standard or if they give more relevant and reliable information, and then they must be disclosed and usually applied retrospectively.",["Same policies each period","Enables comparison over time","Changes must be justified and disclosed"]],
["raj",1,"What is inventory, accounting-wise? Is it the same as what's on my warehouse shelves?","Goods held for sale or used in production.","**Inventory** is goods held for sale in the ordinary course of business, goods in production, and materials to be used in production. It's measured at the lower of cost and net realisable value. It may include goods in transit you already own and exclude goods on your shelves that you hold for someone else (consignment). So the accounting figure and the shelf count can differ.",["Goods held for sale / in production / materials","Lower of cost and NRV","Ownership, not location, decides"]],
// tier 2
["lena",2,"What's a T-account and why do accountants still draw them?","A simple picture of one ledger account.","A **T-account** is a sketch of a ledger account: debits on the left, credits on the right, account name on top. It's a thinking tool for working out entries and balances, for example tracing what happened to cash or receivables during a period. The balance is the difference between the two sides.",["Visual ledger account: debit left, credit right","Used to work out entries and balances","Balance = difference between sides"]],
["lena",2,"What's an account's 'normal balance'?","Which side is it usually on?","The normal balance is the side where increases are recorded: **debit** for assets, expenses and drawings; **credit** for liabilities, equity and revenue. An account showing the opposite balance is unusual and worth checking, for example a credit balance in cash means an overdraft, or a debit balance in payables means you overpaid a supplier.",["Assets/expenses: debit; liabilities/equity/revenue: credit","Normal balance = side of increases","Opposite balance signals something unusual"]],
["clara",2,"Our trial balance balances perfectly, so the books are right, aren't they?","Name errors where debits still equal credits.","Not necessarily. Many errors leave debits equal to credits: **omission** (a transaction not recorded at all), **commission** (right type of account, wrong account, e.g. one customer's payment posted to another), **principle** (expense recorded as an asset), **original entry** (wrong amount on both sides), **complete reversal** (debit and credit swapped), and **compensating errors**. A balanced trial balance only proves arithmetical consistency.",["Balance ≠ correctness","Names several error types not revealed","Only proves arithmetic consistency"]],
["lena",2,"What are adjusting entries and why are they needed at period end?","Accruals, deferrals, depreciation…","Adjusting entries make the accounts follow the **accrual principle** at period end: record **accrued expenses** (work done, not yet invoiced), **accrued revenue** (earned, not yet billed), **prepayments** (paid in advance, partly used), **deferred revenue** (received in advance, partly earned), plus depreciation and allowances for doubtful debts. Without them, revenue and expenses would land in the wrong periods.",["Needed for accrual accounting at period end","Types: accruals, prepayments, deferred revenue, depreciation, allowances","Puts revenue/expenses in the right period"]],
["olena",2,"I sell gift cards at the bakery. Is that revenue when I sell the card?","Have you delivered anything yet?","No. When you sell a gift card you've received cash but haven't delivered bread yet, so you record a **liability** (deferred revenue / contract liability): Dr Cash, Cr Gift card liability. Revenue is recognised when the card is **redeemed**. For cards that are never used ('breakage'), revenue can be recognised in proportion to redemptions if you can reliably estimate it.",["Card sale = liability, not revenue","Revenue on redemption","Breakage for unused cards"]],
["diego",2,"I paid six months of rent in advance in November. Our year ends in December. Is all of it this year's expense?","How much of the period has passed?","No. Only the part covering this year (November and December, two months) is an expense. The remaining four months are a **prepaid expense**, a current asset, because you'll receive the benefit next year. At year-end you'd move the used part to rent expense and leave the rest as prepaid rent.",["Only consumed portion is expense","Remainder is a prepaid asset","Accrual / matching principle"]],
["lena",2,"What are closing entries?","What happens to revenue and expense accounts at year-end?","At year-end, the **temporary accounts** (revenue, expenses, drawings/dividends) are reset to zero and their balances moved to equity (retained earnings or the owner's capital account). That way each year's income statement starts fresh, while balance sheet accounts (permanent accounts) carry over. Software does this automatically, but it explains why profit ends up in equity.",["Reset temporary accounts","Transfer net result to retained earnings/capital","Balance sheet accounts carry forward"]],
["olena",2,"I repaired the oven door for €300 and also added a new steam system for €4,000. Do I treat them the same?","Does the spending maintain the asset or improve it?","No. The **door repair** maintains the oven's existing condition, so it's an **expense** now. The **steam system** improves capacity or extends useful life, so it's **capital expenditure**: added to the asset's cost and depreciated. Rule of thumb: maintenance = expense; enhancement/extension = capitalise.",["Repair/maintenance → expense","Improvement → capitalise and depreciate","Test: does it enhance or extend the asset?"]],
["diego",2,"What is petty cash and how should it be controlled?","Small cash payments, imprest system.","**Petty cash** is a small cash float for minor payments (milk, parcel postage). Under an **imprest system**, the float is a fixed amount; each payment needs a voucher/receipt, and the float is topped up by exactly the total of vouchers. At any time, cash + vouchers should equal the float. Regular counts and separate custody limit theft.",["Small cash float for minor expenses","Imprest system: top up to fixed amount","Vouchers and counts as controls"]],
["raj",2,"A customer returned items worth €200. How does that show up?","Not a negative expense: a reduction of revenue.","A return is recorded in **sales returns** (a contra-revenue account), which reduces gross revenue to **net revenue**. If the customer had paid, you refund cash or credit their receivable; returned goods in sellable condition go back into inventory and reduce cost of sales. Tracking returns separately shows how big the problem is.",["Sales returns reduce revenue (contra-revenue)","Reverse receivable/cash","Returned goods back to inventory if sellable"]],
["diego",2,"Our stock count shows less wine than the system says. How do we account for it?","Shrinkage.","The difference is **inventory shrinkage** (theft, breakage, spillage, recording errors). The inventory records are adjusted down to the physical count, and the difference is recognised as an expense (often within cost of sales). Investigate large or repeated differences: they may indicate control weaknesses.",["Adjust books to physical count","Difference = shrinkage expense","Investigate as a control issue"]],
["lena",2,"Periodic vs perpetual inventory systems: what's the difference?","When is cost of sales calculated?","**Perpetual**: inventory and cost of sales are updated with every purchase and sale (barcode systems), so you always know the book stock. **Periodic**: purchases are recorded, but cost of sales is only worked out at period end: opening stock + purchases − closing stock (from a count). Perpetual gives better control and shows shrinkage when compared with a physical count.",["Perpetual updates with each transaction","Periodic computes COGS at period end","Perpetual better for control"]],
["raj",2,"What's a chart of accounts?","The list of all accounts the business uses.","A **chart of accounts** is the structured list of every account in the ledger, usually numbered by category (assets, liabilities, equity, revenue, expenses). A well-designed one makes reports meaningful (e.g. separating shipping costs from marketing) while staying simple enough to be used consistently. In some countries standard charts are common (e.g. SKR 03/04 in Germany).",["Structured list of all accounts","Grouped/numbered by category","Design affects useful reporting"]],
["priya",2,"A client paid me a €1,500 deposit for a branding project that I'll deliver next month. Revenue now?","Have you performed the work yet?","Not under accrual accounting. The deposit is a **liability** (customer deposit / deferred revenue) until you deliver: Dr Cash, Cr Contract liability €1,500. When you complete and hand over the work, you recognise revenue. If the project is delivered in stages, revenue can be recognised as each stage is completed.",["Deposit = liability","Revenue when work delivered","Staged recognition possible"]],
["olena",2,"My supplier offers me a 'trade discount'. How is that different from a 'cash discount'?","One is on the price, one is for paying early.","A **trade discount** is a reduction in list price (e.g. for buying in bulk); you simply record the purchase at the lower price. A **cash (settlement) discount** is offered for paying early (e.g. 2% if paid in 10 days); it depends on your payment timing. Under IFRS, purchases are recorded net of expected discounts, so they reduce the cost of inventory rather than being 'income'.",["Trade discount reduces the price recorded","Cash discount for early payment","Reduces cost rather than income"]],
["lena",2,"Why is drawing or paying a dividend not on the income statement?","Is it a cost of earning revenue?","Because it's a **distribution to owners**, not a cost of running the business. Profit measures the performance of the business; what owners then do with it (keep it or take it out) is an equity transaction. Dividends and drawings reduce retained earnings/capital directly, shown in the statement of changes in equity.",["Distribution to owners, not an expense","Reduces equity directly","Shown in statement of changes in equity"]],
["clara",2,"What's a suspense account?","Temporary home for unexplained differences.","A **suspense account** temporarily holds amounts you can't yet classify, for example an unexplained difference in the trial balance or a receipt with no reference. It must be cleared by investigating and posting to the correct accounts. A suspense balance at year-end is a red flag in an audit.",["Temporary holding account","Must be investigated and cleared","Year-end balance is a warning sign"]],
["olena",2,"Is the bank loan I took to expand the shop a current or non-current liability?","When is it due?","Split it by due date. The part repayable **within 12 months** of the balance sheet date is a **current liability**; the rest is **non-current**. That's important for liquidity analysis. If you breach a loan covenant so the bank can demand immediate repayment, the whole loan may have to be shown as current.",["Split by 12-month horizon","Current portion vs non-current","Covenant breach can make it all current"]],
["pavlo",2,"I keep grain in my silo to sell later. Is the harvested grain inventory?","Biological assets vs agricultural produce.","Yes. Once harvested, grain is **agricultural produce** and becomes inventory, measured at fair value less costs to sell at the point of harvest under IFRS (IAS 41), which then becomes its cost for inventory purposes. Before harvest, growing crops are **biological assets** with their own rules. For a small farm using local GAAP or tax accounts, simpler cost-based rules may apply.",["Harvested grain = inventory","At harvest measured at fair value less costs to sell (IAS 41)","Growing crops are biological assets"]],
// tier 3
["lena",3,"What's the difference between the direct write-off method and the allowance method for bad debts?","Timing of the expense.","**Direct write-off**: expense recognised only when a specific debt is declared uncollectible. Simple, but it often puts the loss in a later period than the sale, and overstates receivables meanwhile. **Allowance method**: estimate uncollectible amounts at period end and recognise the expense in the same period as the sales, with an allowance reducing receivables. IFRS requires the expected-credit-loss (allowance) approach.",["Direct write-off: expense when debt fails","Allowance: estimate expected losses in same period","Allowance matches and is required under IFRS 9"]],
["lena",3,"We realised a machine will last 8 years instead of the 5 we assumed. Do we restate past years?","Change in estimate vs error.","No. A revised useful life is a **change in accounting estimate**, applied **prospectively**: the remaining book value is depreciated over the new remaining life from now on. Past years aren't restated because the old estimate was reasonable with the information available then. Errors and changes in accounting policy, by contrast, are generally applied retrospectively.",["Change in estimate","Applied prospectively","No restatement unless it's an error or policy change"]],
["clara",3,"We found that last year's inventory was overstated by €50,000 due to a counting error. How do we fix it?","Prior period error.","It's a **material prior-period error**, so it's corrected **retrospectively**: restate the comparative figures as if the error never happened (last year's closing inventory and profit both lower; this year's opening retained earnings reduced). It's disclosed in the notes. You don't just push the €50,000 through this year's profit, which would distort both years.",["Prior-period error","Retrospective restatement of comparatives","Disclose; don't run it through current profit"]],
["adeyemi",3,"A major customer went bankrupt two weeks after our year-end. The accounts aren't signed yet. Does it affect last year's figures?","Adjusting vs non-adjusting events after the reporting period.","Usually yes: it's an **adjusting event**. The bankruptcy provides evidence about conditions that existed at year-end (the receivable was already impaired), so you increase the allowance or write off the receivable in last year's accounts. By contrast, an event arising after year-end (e.g. a fire in the warehouse in January) is **non-adjusting**: disclose it if material, but don't change the figures.",["Events after reporting period","Adjusting: evidence of year-end conditions","Non-adjusting: disclose if material"]],
["adeyemi",3,"What's the difference between a provision and a contingent liability?","Probability and reliable estimate.","A **provision** is a liability of uncertain timing or amount that is recognised: there's a present obligation from a past event, an outflow is **probable** (more likely than not), and it can be **reliably estimated** (e.g. warranty claims). A **contingent liability** is not recognised; it's only disclosed, because the outflow is only possible, or it can't be measured reliably. If remote, nothing is disclosed.",["Provision: present obligation, probable, reliable estimate → recognised","Contingent liability: possible or not measurable → disclosed only","Remote → nothing"]],
["raj",3,"I sell annual software subscriptions paid upfront. How should I recognise the revenue?","Over what period do you deliver?","The service is delivered evenly over 12 months, so revenue is recognised **over time**, typically 1/12 each month. The upfront cash is first recorded as a **contract liability** (deferred revenue), which is released to revenue monthly. Booking it all on day one would overstate early revenue and understate later months.",["Revenue over the subscription period","Upfront cash = deferred revenue","Monthly release"]],
["raj",3,"Shipping terms say 'FOB shipping point'. Why does that matter for my year-end?","When does ownership transfer?","Shipping terms determine **when control passes**. FOB shipping point: the buyer owns goods once they leave the seller, so goods in transit at year-end belong to the **buyer** (seller recognises the sale). FOB destination: ownership passes on arrival, so goods in transit still belong to the **seller**. This affects revenue, receivables and inventory cut-off. (Under modern trade practice, Incoterms define this.)",["Terms determine when control transfers","FOB shipping point: buyer owns in transit","Affects revenue and inventory cut-off"]],
["clara",3,"What's a 'cut-off' test and why do auditors care so much about it?","Transactions in the right period.","**Cut-off** means recording transactions in the **correct period**. Auditors examine sales, purchases and shipments just before and after year-end to check that, e.g., goods shipped on 2 January aren't recorded as December sales. Cut-off errors are a common way, deliberate or not, of shifting profit between years.",["Right transactions in right period","Testing around year-end","Common source of misstatement/manipulation"]],
["olena",3,"My bank account is overdrawn at year-end. Can my accountant just show cash as a negative number?","Overdrafts are liabilities.","Generally an overdraft is a **liability**, shown under current liabilities (e.g. bank overdrafts), not as negative cash, and assets and liabilities shouldn't be offset unless there's a legal right and intention to settle net. In the cash flow statement, some frameworks allow overdrafts repayable on demand to be included in cash and cash equivalents if they're part of everyday cash management.",["Overdraft = current liability","No offsetting without right and intention","Cash flow statement may include it in cash equivalents"]],
["lena",3,"What's the difference between current and non-current assets?","12 months / operating cycle.","**Current assets** are expected to be realised, sold or consumed within 12 months or the normal operating cycle: cash, receivables, inventory, prepayments. **Non-current assets** are held longer: property, equipment, intangibles, long-term investments. The split helps judge liquidity, i.e. what can be turned into cash to pay short-term obligations.",["Current: realised within 12 months/operating cycle","Non-current: long-term","Matters for liquidity analysis"]],
["raj",3,"I bought an accounting software licence for 3 years. Asset or expense?","Is it a resource you control with future benefit?","If you **control** the software (a perpetual or fixed-term licence installed and used by you) and it benefits several years, it's an **intangible asset**, amortised over 3 years. If it's a cloud subscription (SaaS) where you just access the supplier's software, it's usually a **service expense** recognised over the subscription period (prepaid if paid upfront).",["Controlled licence → intangible asset, amortised","SaaS access → service expense over time","Prepayment if paid upfront"]],
["kat",3,"We spend a lot on developing our app. Can we capitalise those costs?","Research vs development under IAS 38.","Under IAS 38, **research** costs are always expensed. **Development** costs must be capitalised once specific criteria are met: technical feasibility, intention and ability to complete and use/sell it, probable future economic benefits, adequate resources, and reliable measurement of costs. Before those criteria are met, costs are expensed. Many startups expense most costs because the criteria are hard to demonstrate early.",["Research always expensed","Development capitalised when criteria met","Criteria list; early-stage often expensed"]],
["diego",3,"We renovated the restaurant interior (leased premises) for €80,000. The lease has 6 years left; the renovation would last 10. Over how long do we depreciate it?","Shorter of useful life and lease term.","**Leasehold improvements** are depreciated over the **shorter** of their useful life and the remaining lease term (unless you're reasonably certain to extend), here 6 years: about €13,333 a year. After the lease ends you can't use the improvements, so spreading the cost over 10 years would overstate assets.",["Capitalise as leasehold improvements","Depreciate over shorter of life and lease term","6 years here"]],
["raj",3,"I sell other brands' products on my site, they ship directly and I take a commission. Do I record the full sale price as revenue?","Principal or agent?","It depends on whether you're **principal** or **agent**. If you **control** the goods before they reach the customer (inventory risk, price-setting, primary responsibility), you're principal and report gross revenue. If you merely arrange the sale for the brand and earn a fee, you're an **agent** and report only your **commission** as revenue. Drop-shipping on a commission basis usually points to agent.",["Principal → gross revenue; agent → net commission","Control indicators: inventory risk, pricing, responsibility","Commission model likely agent"]],
["olena",3,"What's the difference between a 'provision' for repairs and just setting money aside?","Accounting entry vs cash.","Under IFRS you can only recognise a provision if there's a **present obligation** from a past event. Planned future repairs aren't an obligation (you could sell the oven instead), so **no provision**. Setting money aside in a savings account is a cash decision and doesn't create an expense. Some local frameworks historically allowed repair provisions, but IFRS doesn't.",["Provision needs present obligation","Future repairs are not an obligation under IFRS","Saving cash ≠ accounting provision"]],
["lena",3,"How do accounting profit and taxable profit differ?","Different rules, different purposes.","Accounting profit follows financial reporting standards to show economic performance; **taxable profit** follows tax law to collect revenue. Differences arise from items not deductible for tax (some fines, entertainment), items taxed differently (faster tax depreciation), and timing differences. Permanent differences affect the effective tax rate; temporary differences create **deferred tax**.",["Different purposes and rules","Permanent vs temporary differences","Temporary differences → deferred tax"]],
["clara",3,"Why do auditors care about 'related party transactions'?","Not at arm's length.","Transactions with related parties (owners, directors, their families, group companies) may **not be at arm's length**: prices or terms could differ from market, shifting profit or hiding obligations. Standards require disclosure of the relationships and transactions so users can judge their effect. Auditors check they're identified, properly approved and disclosed.",["Related parties may not transact at arm's length","Disclosure requirements","Risk of profit shifting/hidden obligations"]],
["lena",3,"What is 'other comprehensive income'?","Gains and losses that bypass profit or loss.","**OCI** contains certain gains and losses recognised in equity rather than in profit or loss, e.g. revaluation surpluses on property, some fair value changes, foreign currency translation differences, actuarial gains/losses on pensions. Profit + OCI = **total comprehensive income**. The idea is to keep volatile, unrealised or long-term items separate from operating performance.",["Gains/losses outside profit or loss","Examples: revaluation, FX translation, pension remeasurements","Total comprehensive income = profit + OCI"]],
["olena",3,"What's a statement of changes in equity?","Bridges opening and closing equity.","It reconciles **opening and closing equity**: profit or loss, other comprehensive income, owner contributions (new shares/capital), distributions (dividends/drawings), and any restatements. It shows why equity changed, separating performance from transactions with owners.",["Reconciles opening to closing equity","Includes profit, OCI, contributions, distributions","Separates performance from owner transactions"]],
["pavlo",3,"I received an EU subsidy to buy a new irrigation system. Is it income right away?","Government grants related to assets.","No. A grant related to an asset is recognised in profit **over the asset's useful life**, matching the depreciation it helps fund. IAS 20 allows either deducting the grant from the asset's cost (lower depreciation) or showing it as **deferred income** released over the life. Recognising it all at once would overstate profit in the year received.",["Asset-related grant spread over useful life","Deduct from cost or deferred income","Matches the related depreciation"]],
["diego",3,"We give a 1-year guarantee on catering equipment we sell. How do we account for expected repairs?","Warranty provision.","At the time of sale, recognise a **warranty provision** for the expected cost of repairs: Dr Warranty expense / Cr Provision, based on historical claim rates. Actual repairs are then charged against the provision. That matches the warranty cost with the sale that created it.",["Provision at time of sale","Estimate from experience","Matches cost with revenue"]],
// tier 4
["adeyemi",4,"What's a 'constructive obligation'?","Obligations without a contract.","A **constructive obligation** arises from the company's actions, published policies or established practice that create a valid expectation that it will accept certain responsibilities, even without a legal requirement. Example: a retailer that always refunds unhappy customers beyond legal requirements, or a company that publicly commits to clean up contamination. It can require a provision just like a legal obligation.",["Arises from practice/policy/public statement","Creates valid expectation","Can trigger a provision"]],
["adeyemi",4,"Our lawyers think we will probably win a €2m lawsuit against a supplier. Can we book the gain?","Contingent assets.","No. It's a **contingent asset**: not recognised until realisation is **virtually certain**. If an inflow is probable, it's disclosed in the notes. This asymmetry with contingent liabilities (recognised when probable) reflects prudence: gains need stronger evidence than losses.",["Contingent asset not recognised","Disclosed if probable","Recognise only when virtually certain"]],
["lena",4,"What's the difference between a change in accounting policy and a change in estimate?","Principle vs measurement judgement.","A **policy** change alters the principles, bases or rules applied, e.g. switching from cost model to revaluation model, and is applied **retrospectively** (comparatives restated). An **estimate** change revises a judgement about a measurement, e.g. useful life, bad-debt percentage, warranty rate, and is applied **prospectively**. When unclear, it's treated as a change in estimate.",["Policy: principles → retrospective","Estimate: measurement judgement → prospective","Examples of each"]],
["adeyemi",4,"We're selling one of our divisions next year. How is that presented?","Discontinued operations / held for sale.","If the division is a separate major line of business and is classified as **held for sale** (available for immediate sale, sale highly probable within a year), it's presented as a **discontinued operation**: its after-tax result appears as a single line separate from continuing operations, with comparatives restated. Its assets and liabilities are shown separately, measured at the lower of carrying amount and fair value less costs to sell, and depreciation stops.",["Held for sale criteria","Single line for discontinued operations; comparatives restated","Lower of carrying amount and FV less costs to sell; no depreciation"]],
["yuki",4,"Some companies revalue their property every year, others keep it at cost. How does that affect comparison?","Cost model vs revaluation model.","Under IAS 16 a company may choose the **cost model** (cost less depreciation) or the **revaluation model** (fair value, with increases going to OCI as a revaluation surplus). Revaluing firms show higher assets and equity and higher depreciation, so lower profit and lower ROA/ROE versus a cost-model peer with identical properties. Analysts adjust for this before comparing ratios.",["Cost vs revaluation model","Revaluation: higher assets/equity, higher depreciation, gains to OCI","Ratios not comparable without adjustment"]],
["clara",4,"Can a company reverse an inventory write-down?","IAS 2.","Yes. Under IAS 2, if net realisable value recovers (e.g. prices rebound) for inventory still held, the earlier write-down is **reversed**, but only up to the original cost. The reversal reduces the cost of sales in that period. (Under US GAAP, inventory write-downs are generally not reversed.)",["Reversal allowed under IAS 2","Capped at original cost","US GAAP differs"]],
["yuki",4,"What's 'substance over form'?","Economic reality vs legal wording.","Transactions should be accounted for according to their **economic substance**, not just their legal form. Example: a 'sale' of an asset with an obligation to buy it back at a fixed price is really a **secured loan**, so the asset stays on the balance sheet and a liability is recognised. This principle prevents companies from dressing up transactions to look better.",["Economic substance over legal form","Example: sale and repurchase = financing","Prevents misleading presentation"]],
["clara",4,"What is 'window dressing'?","Making year-end figures look better temporarily.","Window dressing is arranging transactions around the balance sheet date to make the position look better than it normally is, e.g. delaying supplier payments to show more cash, repaying a loan just before year-end and re-borrowing just after, or pushing extra sales into December. It may be legal but misleading; auditors look for unusual year-end transactions that reverse shortly afterward.",["Temporary year-end improvement","Examples: loan repay/reborrow, delayed payments, pushed sales","Auditors test transactions around year-end"]],
["adeyemi",4,"What's the difference between capitalising borrowing costs and expensing them?","Qualifying assets.","Under IAS 23, borrowing costs directly attributable to building or acquiring a **qualifying asset** (one that takes a substantial time to get ready, like a new factory) are **capitalised** into the asset's cost during construction, then depreciated with it. Other borrowing costs are expensed. Capitalising raises current profit and asset values.",["Qualifying assets take substantial time to prepare","Directly attributable interest capitalised during construction","Otherwise expensed"]],
["yuki",4,"What's 'channel stuffing'?","Inflating sales by over-shipping to distributors.","Channel stuffing means pushing excessive inventory onto distributors or retailers near period end, often with generous return rights, extended payment terms or side deals, to report higher sales now. Revenue should not be recognised if control hasn't really transferred or if large returns are expected. Warning signs: receivables growing faster than sales and a spike in returns next quarter.",["Excess shipments to distributors to inflate sales","Revenue recognition issues: returns, control","Red flags: receivables growth, later returns"]],
["clara",4,"A client signs a 'bill-and-hold' arrangement: they've paid but we keep the goods in our warehouse. Can we book the sale?","Has control passed?","Only if strict conditions are met: the arrangement must be **substantive** (the customer asked for it), the goods must be **separately identified** as the customer's, **ready for transfer**, and the seller must not be able to use or redirect them. If these are met, control has passed and revenue can be recognised; otherwise it's deferred. Bill-and-hold is a classic area for abuse.",["Revenue only if control has transferred","Conditions: substantive reason, identified, ready, can't redirect","High-risk area for manipulation"]],
["lena",4,"What's the difference between accrual accounting's 'matching' and the modern 'asset/liability' approach?","What drives recognition?","Older thinking centred on **matching**: recognise expenses in the same period as the related revenue. Modern frameworks (IFRS Conceptual Framework) are **balance-sheet-driven**: recognise assets and liabilities when definitions are met; income and expenses are changes in them. Matching still happens in practice (depreciation, warranties), but you can't create an asset or liability just to smooth profit.",["Matching vs asset/liability definitions","Modern framework is balance-sheet based","No artificial deferrals to smooth profit"]],
["adeyemi",4,"We hold shares of a supplier as a strategic investment. How do fair value changes show up?","IFRS 9 equity investments.","Under IFRS 9, equity investments are measured at **fair value**. By default changes go to **profit or loss**. For strategic investments not held for trading, the company can make an irrevocable election at initial recognition to present changes in **OCI**; then gains are never recycled to profit even when sold (dividends still go to profit).",["Measured at fair value","Default: changes through profit or loss","OCI election for non-trading equity, no recycling"]],
["yuki",4,"A company's revenue grew 20% but receivables grew 60%. Should I worry?","What does it suggest about revenue quality?","Yes, it's a red flag. Receivables growing much faster than sales can mean **looser credit terms** to win sales, **collection problems**, or **aggressive revenue recognition** (sales booked before they're really earned, or channel stuffing). Check DSO trends, the allowance for doubtful debts, revenue recognition notes, and cash flow from operations versus net income.",["Receivables outgrowing sales = red flag","Possible causes: credit terms, collection issues, aggressive recognition","Check DSO, allowances, CFO vs profit"]],
["clara",4,"What are accrual-based 'earnings management' techniques and how can they be spotted?","Discretionary estimates.","Management can shift profit between periods through **discretionary accruals**: optimistic useful lives, low bad-debt or warranty provisions, releasing 'cookie jar' reserves, capitalising costs that should be expensed, aggressive revenue timing. Signs: profits consistently beating targets by small amounts, big gaps between net income and operating cash flow, frequent changes in estimates, and unusual year-end entries.",["Using estimates/accruals to shift profit","Examples: provisions, useful lives, capitalisation, reserves","Signals: profit vs CFO gap, estimate changes, just-beating targets"]],
// tier 5
["yuki",5,"Why do analysts compare net income with operating cash flow over several years?","Accrual ratio / earnings quality.","Over time, profits and operating cash flows should track each other. Persistently higher net income than CFO means profits rest heavily on **accruals** (growing receivables, inventory, capitalised costs), which are more prone to estimation error or manipulation and often reverse. High-quality earnings are backed by cash. Analysts compute accrual ratios to flag firms at risk of future earnings disappointment.",["Earnings quality: profits should convert to cash","Persistent NI > CFO = high accruals","Accruals tend to reverse; risk signal"]],
["clara",5,"What's 'off-balance-sheet financing' and why did standards crack down on it?","Obligations hidden from the balance sheet.","It's structuring transactions so that liabilities don't appear on the balance sheet: operating leases (before IFRS 16), unconsolidated special-purpose entities, sale-and-repurchase deals, factoring with recourse. It makes companies look less leveraged. After scandals like Enron, standards tightened: IFRS 16 put most leases on balance sheet, and IFRS 10 consolidation rules are based on control, not legal ownership percentages.",["Liabilities kept off balance sheet","Examples: leases, SPEs, factoring with recourse","Reforms: IFRS 16, control-based consolidation"]],
["adeyemi",5,"Our auditor raised a 'material uncertainty related to going concern'. What does that mean for our accounts?","Disclosure vs basis of preparation.","It means there are events or conditions casting **significant doubt** on the company's ability to continue, but management still considers the going-concern basis appropriate (e.g. refinancing is likely). The accounts are prepared normally but must **disclose** the uncertainty, and the audit report includes a separate section highlighting it. If liquidation were inevitable, a different basis would be required. Lenders and suppliers often react to such disclosures.",["Significant doubt but going concern still used","Must be disclosed; auditor highlights it","If liquidation inevitable, different basis"]],
["lena",5,"Why does IFRS forbid LIFO, while US GAAP allows it?","Think about balance sheet relevance and taxes.","IFRS bans **LIFO** because, with rising prices, it leaves very old costs in inventory on the balance sheet (unrepresentative values) and allows profit manipulation by timing purchases (LIFO liquidation). The US allows it largely for **tax** reasons: the LIFO conformity rule means firms using LIFO for tax must use it in their books, and LIFO lowers taxable profit when prices rise.",["LIFO leaves outdated costs in inventory","Manipulation through purchase timing","US tax conformity rule explains its survival"]],
["yuki",5,"How can a company 'manage' its earnings using restructuring provisions?","Big bath accounting.","A **big bath**: in a bad year (often under a new CEO), the company books large restructuring provisions and write-offs, sometimes overestimating them. Future costs are pre-charged, and later, unused provisions are released, boosting future profits. Standards limit this: restructuring provisions require a detailed formal plan and a valid expectation among those affected, and only direct costs qualify.",["Overstated one-off charges in a bad year","Release later to inflate profits","IAS 37 restrictions on restructuring provisions"]],
["clara",5,"What is the 'neutrality' qualitative characteristic, and how does it relate to prudence?","Without bias.","**Neutrality** means information is presented without bias: not slanted, weighted or manipulated to make a favourable or unfavourable impression. IFRS supports it with **prudence** in the sense of caution under uncertainty, not systematic understatement. Deliberately understating assets or overstating liabilities (hidden reserves) violates neutrality just as much as overstating profit.",["Neutral = free from bias","Prudence = caution, not systematic understatement","Hidden reserves violate neutrality"]],
["lena",5,"Why do some argue historical cost is better than fair value, and vice versa?","Relevance vs reliability.","**Historical cost** is objective, verifiable and less volatile, but may be outdated and less relevant (land bought decades ago). **Fair value** is more relevant for decisions and reflects current conditions, but can be subjective (level 3 estimates without market prices), volatile and procyclical. Standards mix both: fair value where markets are active and relevant (financial instruments), cost for most operating assets.",["Historical cost: reliable but possibly outdated","Fair value: relevant but subjective/volatile","Standards use a mixed model"]],
// tier 6
["clara",6,"What's the fair value hierarchy, and why do auditors focus on level 3?","Inputs used to measure fair value.","IFRS 13 ranks fair value inputs: **Level 1**: quoted prices in active markets for identical assets; **Level 2**: other observable inputs (similar assets, yield curves); **Level 3**: unobservable inputs based on the entity's own assumptions (models for private equity, complex derivatives). Level 3 values involve significant judgement and potential bias, so they need more disclosures and audit scrutiny.",["Levels 1/2/3 by observability of inputs","Level 3 = unobservable, model-based","High judgement → more disclosure and audit focus"]],
["yuki",6,"Two airlines report the same profit, but one capitalises engine overhauls and the other expenses them. How would you compare them?","Normalise accounting choices.","Adjust them to a common basis. Capitalising overhauls raises current profit and assets, shifting cost into future depreciation. To compare, either expense the capitalised overhauls for the first airline or capitalise the other's. Also compare **cash flow measures** (EBITDA minus maintenance capex, free cash flow), which are less affected by the choice, and check disclosures on useful lives and component accounting.",["Accounting choices distort comparability","Restate to a common policy","Use cash-based measures less sensitive to policy"]],
["adeyemi",6,"How does consolidation work at a high level, and what's goodwill's role in it?","Parent + subsidiaries as one economic entity.","Consolidated statements present the parent and its **controlled** subsidiaries as a single economic entity: add assets, liabilities, income and expenses line by line, eliminate intra-group balances, transactions and unrealised profits, and replace the parent's investment with the subsidiary's net assets. At acquisition, goodwill = consideration (+ non-controlling interest + previously held stake) − fair value of identifiable net assets. **Non-controlling interests** show the part of equity not owned by the parent.",["Control → line-by-line consolidation","Eliminate intra-group items","Goodwill and non-controlling interests at acquisition"]],
["clara",6,"What are 'key audit matters' and what do they tell investors?","Section in listed-company audit reports.","**Key audit matters** are the issues the auditor judged most significant in the audit: areas of high risk, significant judgement (impairment, revenue recognition, provisions, valuations), or significant events. The report explains why each matters and how the auditor addressed it. They don't change the opinion but show investors where the accounts are most judgement-heavy.",["Most significant audit areas","Explains why and how addressed","Signals judgement-heavy areas; not a qualification"]],
["lena",6,"Why might a company's equity be negative even though it's healthy and profitable?","Buybacks, dividends, accounting conservatism.","Negative book equity can result from **large buybacks and dividends** exceeding accumulated profits (common in mature, cash-generative firms), from intangible value (brands, customer relationships) not being recognised on the balance sheet, or from leveraged recapitalisations. Book equity measures accumulated accounting capital, not economic value; such firms may still have strong cash flows and market value.",["Distributions exceed retained profits","Internally generated intangibles not recognised","Book equity ≠ economic value"]],
["yuki",6,"What is 'segment reporting' and why does it matter for analysis?","Breaking down a group's results.","IFRS 8 requires listed companies to report results by **operating segments**, as reviewed by the chief operating decision maker (business lines, regions). It reveals which parts generate profit and growth, where capital is invested, and cross-subsidies hidden in consolidated totals. Analysts use it to build sum-of-the-parts valuations and spot weak divisions.",["Results by operating segment (management view)","Reveals profitability/growth by unit","Supports sum-of-the-parts analysis"]],
["clara",6,"Revenue grows steadily but the auditor found that 30% of Q4 sales were reversed in January. What's going on?","Think about cut-off and side agreements.","Large reversals right after year-end suggest **premature or fictitious revenue**: goods shipped without firm orders, sales with undisclosed return rights or side letters, or bill-and-hold abuses. The Q4 revenue likely didn't meet recognition criteria (control hadn't transferred, or significant returns were expected). Auditors would expand cut-off testing, confirm terms directly with customers and check for side agreements; the accounts may need restating.",["Post-year-end reversals signal improper Q4 revenue","Causes: side agreements, return rights, premature shipment","Audit response: cut-off testing, confirmations, restatement"]]
]);
